Done. `router.py` now applies the step 3 correction and `NOTES.md` explains it.

**→ What changed.** A new eligibility detector in `router.py` sends age, child, residency and country questions to account_security from any department. It runs after the PIN rule and before the exchange-rate rule, so steps 1 and 2 keep working.

**→ How it decides.** The message must contain no money-movement, currency, fee, delivery, dispute or card-feature word. Then one of these fires: an age phrase, a child word next to account or card, "abroad" next to sign up or open, or a place word plus an availability word such as support, offer, work in, get a card, or where are you.

**→ Verification caveat.** Python execution needs an approval this session cannot grant, so I could not run the test script. I hand-traced all 32 evidence rows and 36 extra cases instead, including step 1 and step 2 regressions. The test script is at <tmp> and takes the task directory as its only argument.

**→ Known risk.** Place-plus-availability is the loosest rule. A card question like "will my card work in Spain" moves to account_security, which matches the evidence pattern but may differ on the hidden set.
